  "label": "Biomolecular Model Validation Practices",
  "related": [
    "zero-dna-mirrordna-8a09",
    "models-brain-b963",
    "long-zero-2365"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [
    "ml-ai"
  ],
  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Preprints audit antimicrobial peptide benchmarks and co-folding confidence scores, questioning whether reported performance reflects biological validity.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether reported model performance reflects the biological property of interest. For protein-ligand co-folding, one preprint reports that confidence scores barely outperform random selection and proposes the CORE Score, which relies on structural agreement among predictions. A second preprint shows that antimicrobial peptide benchmark outcomes depend on evaluation design: cluster-disjoint versus random splitting and negative-set choice alter the performance of frozen ESM-2 classifiers. The published PertiNet study presents a multimodal protein-protein interaction model that fuses sequence, structure and GO annotations via modality tokens. Its available summary describes architecture only, so it is treated as modeling context rather than validation evidence. What is new is the shift from headline accuracy toward reliability estimates and evaluation design that bear on downstream use.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
